WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.020
Two tasks train a model briefly.

2
00:00:03.370 --> 00:00:05.370
DeTok trains an image tokenizer

3
00:00:05.435 --> 00:00:06.338
as a denoiser

4
00:00:06.402 --> 00:00:08.467
it hides image patches and mixes

5
00:00:08.531 --> 00:00:10.015
noise into the latents

6
00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:11.757
and the decoder must still

7
00:00:11.822 --> 00:00:13.370
rebuild the clean image.

8
00:00:13.720 --> 00:00:16.409
But the judged run is only sixteen updates

9
00:00:16.409 --> 00:00:19.160
at a learning rate of ten to the minus six.

10
00:00:19.510 --> 00:00:21.837
Most runs ended at the reference's

11
00:00:21.905 --> 00:00:22.863
image quality

12
00:00:22.863 --> 00:00:25.190
so wall time decided most scores.

13
00:00:25.540 --> 00:00:26.455
Opus, for one

14
00:00:26.521 --> 00:00:29.398
reproduced the model's roughly three hundred

15
00:00:29.463 --> 00:00:32.210
forty-three million random numbers faster

16
00:00:32.210 --> 00:00:33.060
bit for bit.

17
00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:35.831
DyT replaces LayerNorm with

18
00:00:35.912 --> 00:00:39.320
one element-wise function: a learned tanh.

19
00:00:39.670 --> 00:00:42.774
LayerNorm uses a token's mean and variance

20
00:00:42.774 --> 00:00:45.590
so one outlier rescales every feature.

21
00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:48.340
DyT treats each feature alone

22
00:00:48.340 --> 00:00:50.820
and caps the outlier near one.

23
00:00:51.170 --> 00:00:53.170
With no statistics to compute

24
00:00:53.170 --> 00:00:54.370
it can be faster.

25
00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:56.402
The reference and most runs

26
00:00:56.465 --> 00:00:57.524
stayed at chance

27
00:00:57.524 --> 00:00:59.580
twenty percent for five classes

28
00:00:59.580 --> 00:01:01.200
so the race was on speed.

29
00:01:01.550 --> 00:01:02.776
At equal accuracy

30
00:01:02.844 --> 00:01:05.773
the score stops at seventy once the step is

31
00:01:05.841 --> 00:01:08.430
about one point one nine times faster.

32
00:01:09.010 --> 00:01:10.610
Three models passed.

33
00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:13.128
GPT-6 Astra also reached the

34
00:01:13.205 --> 00:01:15.760
top score of seventy, and failed.

35
00:01:16.110 --> 00:01:18.421
The score times only the training step

36
00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:20.199
but the pass rule also checks

37
00:01:20.258 --> 00:01:21.147
inference time

38
00:01:21.147 --> 00:01:22.925
and on one workload of three

39
00:01:22.984 --> 00:01:24.406
its inference was slower

40
00:01:24.466 --> 00:01:25.710
than the reference's.

41
00:01:26.290 --> 00:01:28.850
The last task is Polar Express.

42
00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:31.233
A matrix's polar factor keeps

43
00:01:31.303 --> 00:01:32.776
its singular vectors

44
00:01:32.776 --> 00:01:35.440
but sets every singular value to one.

45
00:01:35.790 --> 00:01:37.060
An odd polynomial

46
00:01:37.060 --> 00:01:39.529
applied with matrix products only

47
00:01:39.529 --> 00:01:42.350
acts on each singular value separately.

48
00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:45.278
The classical Newton–Schulz iteration

49
00:01:45.348 --> 00:01:47.020
applies one fixed cubic.

50
00:01:47.370 --> 00:01:49.679
Polar Express picks, for each step

51
00:01:49.745 --> 00:01:52.121
the best polynomial for the interval

52
00:01:52.187 --> 00:01:53.770
the previous steps left.

53
00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:56.915
In our toy, starting from one thousandth

54
00:01:56.983 --> 00:01:59.710
Newton–Schulz still has an error of zero

55
00:01:59.778 --> 00:02:01.960
point nine four after ten steps.

56
00:02:02.310 --> 00:02:04.141
The greedy schedule is down to

57
00:02:04.202 --> 00:02:05.910
three point five millionths.

58
00:02:06.490 --> 00:02:07.910
Pause and predict.

59
00:02:07.989 --> 00:02:10.197
Polar Express is in the same

60
00:02:10.276 --> 00:02:12.090
field as KV Compaction.

61
00:02:12.440 --> 00:02:14.920
How many of the sixteen models passed it?

62
00:02:18.470 --> 00:02:19.350
Fourteen.

63
00:02:19.700 --> 00:02:22.124
It has an exact oracle, the SVD

64
00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:24.169
an optimal polynomial from

65
00:02:24.245 --> 00:02:25.912
a classical algorithm

66
00:02:25.912 --> 00:02:28.260
and a check that needs no GPU.

67
00:02:28.610 --> 00:02:30.933
KV Compaction has none of these

68
00:02:30.933 --> 00:02:32.530
and nobody passed it.

69
00:02:33.610 --> 00:02:35.831
That ends the Machine Learning lecture

70
00:02:35.831 --> 00:02:36.970
with three lessons.

71
00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:38.958
Every workload decides

72
00:02:38.958 --> 00:02:42.019
Opus ran a hundred and eight times faster

73
00:02:42.019 --> 00:02:44.440
and still failed on two articles.

74
00:02:44.790 --> 00:02:46.997
Fit the method to the measurement

75
00:02:46.997 --> 00:02:49.139
short runs left speed to decide

76
00:02:49.139 --> 00:02:51.670
and averaging suited the image scores.

77
00:02:52.020 --> 00:02:54.757
And training data must be exactly right

78
00:02:54.757 --> 00:02:57.220
because its mistakes fail silently.

79
00:02:57.570 --> 00:02:59.838
The interactive notes for this lecture

80
00:02:59.898 --> 00:03:01.330
have every task in full.

81
00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:05.040
Next time: Lecture 8, databases.
